	Language revitalization is not merely the documentation of a language, its lexicon, and its grammar, its usage, etc. Such documentation efforts are often a necessary part of language revitalization, especially when a given language community is no longer socially connected such that there is regular conversational exchange between members in the language belonging to that community. In such cases, the glue that keeps phonology and grammar consistent across families and enclaves tends to erode, and resources like dictionaries and grammars can help provide points of coordination. But, the production of these resources must be linked to an uptake on the part of language learners in order to count as revitalizing a language, on my conception. Producing these kinds of resources alone is what I would consider to be the terminal end of language documentation, with intermediary databases and research material generated along the way.
	Not only do I take language revitalization to differ from language documentation, but I also take it to differ in certain respects from the notion of language preservation, as well. Language preservation, like language documentation, is often deployed in contexts concerning language endangerment and this is a natural context for such kind of projects to emerge. However, I see there being some room for the concept of language preservation to come loose from this context in some cases. I think it that certain prescriptive efforts to maintain the continuance of existing linguistic rules and features of a particular language can be admitted under a certain conception of language preservation. For example, The Académie Française's efforts to standardize and control the character of expressions in the French language could be considered a certain species of language preservation, although we would agree that the French language is not suffering from any status of endangerment by any usual metrics of linguistic health.
	Not only can it be uncoupled from contexts of language decline, but I also take the idea of language preservation to imply a mere continuance of whatever level of language use a given language is currently at. While it might be the case that people, including linguists working in the field, might at least at times use the term 'language preservation' to stand for things which on my understanding count properly as revitalization, I will use the term 'language preservation' here as a label for certain kinds of efforts that stop short of rising to the level of full-blown revitalization. That is to say that language preservation, as I understand it here, could go no further than language documentation efforts and not be disqualified from counting as such, but it would not count as language revitalization.
	There are then two key differences between language documentation and preservation, and language revitalization, as I see it. One defining part of language revitalization is that it involves a tight connection to contexts of language endangerment. I view language revitalization as constituting a response to a given language's decline and as being an attempt to correct or reverse the trajectory of that language's decline. Language endangerment, then, is a necessary first condition on the project of language revitaliztion; it does not make sense to talk about revitalizing a language that does not exhibit any symptoms of a steady decline in number of speakers.
	The other respect in which language revitalization differs from the other two notions is, again, in contrast to language documentation, that there is at least concerted effort to create uptake of the language by new speakers. A central feature of revitalization, then, is the solicitation or propagation of candidate language learners, who, given the circumstances of an existent situation of language endangerment, will then necessarily be second-language learners.
	These two contrasting features of revitalization so far serve only to differentiate it from language preservation and language documentation. What results, at present, then, is a rather minimal understanding of language revitalization. There are many different methods of realizing such a project, and many different ways of conceptualizing its conditions of success or failure in varying degrees. For example, one popular model for realizing the goal of propagating new language speakers is to introduce language classes into existing institutional education for a linguistic community's youth, i.e. classes in primary and secondary schools. In an already established context of compulsory education, how effective additional language curriculum is at getting sustained and enthusiastic uptake from students may vary and depend on many other factors. This looks quite different from another popular model of the adult immersion programs, in which adults self-select into rigorous study of the language, often realized through a master-apprentice style pedagogical setting, where language learners are paired with various fluent speakers and language transmission proceeds via one-on-one instruction and conversation.
	Other models and combinations thereof exist as options for realizing the goals of language revitalization. Here I will not commit to any specifics about what kind of programme of realization any revitalization project needs to take. I will also not include in my definition of revitalization any concrete targets or goals that actual projects will necessarily need to articulate and aim for in order to have momentum and metrics of success. As Hale & Hinton state in The Green Book of Language Revitalization, "[something about how projects will vary widely in their goals etc. depending on what resources they are working with.]" I thus strive here only to put forward a minimal definition which will be capable of canvassing the variations found in actual projects:
	DEFINITION OF LANGUAGE REVITALIZATION
	To revitalize a language A is to make attempts to reverse an existing trend of decline in usage of A, by way of intentional interventions which establish and actualize pathways for the transmission of A-competence or A-fluency to pools of candidate A-language-learners.